---
name: grill-me
description: Interview the user relentlessly about a plan, design, or open decision queue until every material branch is resolved, one decision at a time, with concrete trade-offs and real applied examples. Use when the user wants to stress-test a plan, mentions "grill me" — or whenever a turn is about to end handing open D[n] decisions to the user. Every D[n] block follows the clarification-plan contract in full (numeric example arguments pass through, e.g. "4 2").
---

Interview me relentlessly about every aspect of this plan until we reach a
shared understanding. Walk down each branch of the design tree, resolving
dependencies between decisions one-by-one. **Ask the questions one at a time.**

If a question can be answered by exploring the codebase, docs, or real data —
explore instead of asking.

## The contract lives in `clarification-plan` — load it, then interview

Before writing any D[n] block, **invoke the `clarification-plan` skill and
apply its contract in full**: the anti-shallowness gate (a bug requires root
cause before options), the single decision report (MEASURED vs ESTIMATED
progress, full inventory of every decision blocking execution), the
`Evidence:`/`Unblocks:` lines, applied good/bad examples bound to real
entities, mandatory Option C when A/B are insufficient, and a mandatory
`Recommended answer` per block. Numeric arguments pass through unchanged
(`/grill-me 4 2` ≡ `/clarification-plan 4 2`).

This skill does not restate that contract — it adds the interview protocol on
top of it. If the two ever seem to disagree, `clarification-plan` wins.

## When this skill fires (mandatory, not optional)

1. The user asks to be grilled ("grill me", "stress-test this plan").
2. An orchestration gate requires clarification of a material decision.
3. **A turn is about to end with any open D[n] queue handed to the user** —
   including "decisions this plan does not make", audit pendencies, and
   deferred choices. Dry questions ("A or B?") and dry handoffs ("awaiting
   your decision" + one-line bullets) are both forbidden: they transfer raw
   ambiguity. A reviewer verdict about the executable plan does NOT cover the
   D[n] queue — that queue is material subjectivity by definition. The
   `clarification-stop-gate.py` Stop hook enforces this mechanically on both
   the question path and the handoff path.

## Interview protocol

- One question per turn, each carried by a complete D[n] block ending in
  `**Recommended answer:**` and `**Question:** [one question only]`.
- Order questions by dependency: ask first the decision whose answer can
  eliminate branches of the others.
- After each answer, record the decision, its rationale, and the branches
  eliminated (in the active run state and/or the plan document). Never
  re-ask what is recorded.
- Ask the next question only after resolving dependencies of the current
  branch.
- Finish only when every material branch is resolved or explicitly assumed;
  state residual risks and deferred decisions — each deferred decision as a
  full D[n] block, never as a bare bullet.
